<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khirbet el-Qom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khirbet el-Qom is an archaeological site in the hills west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, where tombs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> were investigated by William Dever in 1967. A burial inscription of the 8th century BCE found there asks blessing by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> and by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>. With the texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuntillet-ajrud">Kuntillet Ajrud</a>, it is key evidence for the religion practised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> before the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khirbet-el-qom.jpg" alt="Khirbet el-Qom" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: פעמי-עליון · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D7%9B%D7%AA%D7%95%D7%91%D7%AA_%D7%A7%D7%91%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94_%D7%9E%D7%97%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%91%D7%AA_%D7%90%D7%9C-%D7%9B%D7%95%D7%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
